{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-122643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=y\\,i-xy\\,j+(z+1)\\,k$\n- $R(t)=(t+1)\\,i-(3t-1)\\,j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Develope