 assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122697" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122697" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122697" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122697" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $G(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$\n- $H(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $C(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x+y}$\n- $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y),7{x}^{3}⟩$\n- $E(x,y,z)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+z),\\operatorname{tan}⁡z,\\frac{1}{x-y}⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne y,\\,z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $N(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},\\sqrt{x+y}⟩$\n- $P(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x-2y},\\sqrt{3-x},3{z}^{3}⟩$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=⟨-1,2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y+z)⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,3-x\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,2x-y+z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $D(x,y,z)=5\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(y+x)\\,k$\n- $E(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-\\sqrt{y}\\,j$\n- $G(x,y,z)=x(y+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y-z)\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $D(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $G(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,y-z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/237dbec35d2ea6556014373efccbf1d34c31404fde3a015feb3c433056c63a3a.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/606f516cb05ba0922cc63cd672a0df864bea121d9c50982a50d3c6f61465f3bf.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/c61552b7b9d8c24e438a8fa2d0a9248a3938c8e3512ca4a012d8b3342dd47a0f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/e1d9695fa4da169a7ec9c1f3ff20db320212bce34047fc13bb27028aadc95d79.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122518" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}\\,j\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,x>{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator of the exponent vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>0}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x>1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x\\ne 1}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0),\\,x\\ge y}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<y}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}"] ["math" "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}}\\,i+\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-x>0\\,⟹\\,y>x.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/c78759bc8f9926159065129e00c1fc8b4341ab83ad40cc87e67a2e82b3e6b089.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2},$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/bdf02ff330f256327d0c9f1147c388b25184610b36ea7ab77bb2c570ec3be19a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/0d3180a60e33c05e904c8fc00fdec4197510198fef3de3fa8c7b3549609205f6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/59ec29347dea9437c8217a208bde0a8b200e37fcbce9c223d0358011a0685297.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/a1ee85bca08b21494e6f1f47de3abcbd71d2d01117795ef91d8ee3e038b36271.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/cb10aa8f4d930ec7422da7dedfa0650244dceb01afe7cc849ed2bb16ffd3e44d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/fadd7ae69d155ebf23586b88369da8f6624b1919942dad543917592f2d3c195c.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122677" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/9f5f11a4ca31cb796f5a95a1541aa946e24fd1b7079ab8069e5743d41d92d764.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/95e8d137f90135de1727451fbc96ddfcc325e9e4461054615c91743a4a9ea54a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90b149214289e53e90a80cf9aa0ee968b30344a501df39857731a7529a54c2a7"} {"question" "q-122671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/0e6843335d08bc42a95a5801aa683aeb2a043df0cd7edc03ffbc36a9385fee2a.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/26709ab7fa3cf1962ff9dca9be510cf5c24f3568f82347a8349e77dc5095fb4c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/4a15607520f8ef05913aab5d1724c8b3158457e56d455b6eb756707263494308.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/a3884061e6c5f8b5310a8b0bcf06ac844b12b418497d9f6e5b3beced6147d964.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "q-122671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/state.json" "file_sha256" "7ecaade75e875d2007b986bca9f01766a32e4d02b3866023222afb1159a6a7cc"} {"question" "e-9465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/example-9465.json" "file_sha256" "7da1f2d1ffb116b2ae6c524d4731d5ec894bc9ca895659956e98c0a7d5b0056d"} {"question" "e-9465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/example-9465.json" "file_sha256" "7da1f2d1ffb116b2ae6c524d4731d5ec894bc9ca895659956e98c0a7d5b0056d"} {"question" "e-9468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\n- $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/example-9468.json" "file_sha256" "bb1f9a3bc2dce2a9f97e73d8556c084408e86d0169e25db33eddf67bed304a22"} {"question" "e-9468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\:|\\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/example-9468.json" "file_sha256" "bb1f9a3bc2dce2a9f97e73d8556c084408e86d0169e25db33eddf67bed304a22"} {"question" "e-9466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/example-9466.json" "file_sha256" "99429c590295cacc62da356805a44f5d0f450f8b52a8008c2f11182e91bf6f1f"} {"question" "e-9466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y} .\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/d4a418bc0dbfc61c0e55c494db51ee0fa8125b6b7056ccbe4e32ddca82574dc2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_2,$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/242903cc94103f8bb9cdc190a229fe7e86395f44600e8318d728fea3bc339385.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by \n\n$$\nD =  D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/f02ca21443a81cdb039a3d97bab0b0f1036219bb89ecbdaa32386cbc4e5da6e3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/example-9466.json" "file_sha256" "99429c590295cacc62da356805a44f5d0f450f8b52a8008c2f11182e91bf6f1f"} {"question" "q-122465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "93238ac67f09ef2666247522db1f728c208add278ea3d0d9db55d5065af97927" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "Using the source's definition allowing a domain subset of R², G maps R² to R² and H maps {(x,y):y>0} to R². K instead takes three spatial coordinates and returns a three-component vector. Thus I and II only, original option b. The authentic worked solution explicitly agrees, and saved grading reports Correct. The stored I only key wrongly excludes the permitted restricted-domain field H." "bindings" {"problem" "7afc74efd53d0f093121e158c3a0c73845743c0188e019b285fadfdc073b3b85" "worked_solution" "cd7679d1995561d237466496ba0abfef5870a505b7042ca3f2f21f7f1d37d16c" "original_field" "4174794484ed11230b922f9e9cb6784bd161ab0a0c7c017e56eb2ab0afa99b7b" "reviewed_field" "64f4877d6457b54bd8e160ce7f08cecc89c6f340dd8f5449c18c65f1c2c2d221" "raw_widget" "01f759e4d6dbbd376c2584fe1a936e00444822a6b9884463f2c2d087d20917b6"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-122465-before.png" "sha256" "16b7c0dd9677f339cf57f03813c42d21b104fa025e6305167ea6b7e6ff70d991"} {"path" "history-q-122465.png" "sha256" "dc7ddab923f8392d2d0d68926ec38df1338ab790a2c6b8b7857b01bf66ca8f0a"} {"path" "q-122465-after.json" "sha256" "b5091dd97be77bb06f2641dc27e1e6fc59ec7792eb2c9f684ac95725df6ccd35"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "0aa1bb836b120f12cf63fe43f86e92beabce23e77bcdac0377887d3d5a689677"}]} {"question" "q-122465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "93238ac67f09ef2666247522db1f728c208add278ea3d0d9db55d5065af97927" "rationale" "Using the source's definition allowing a domain subset of R², G maps R² to R² and H maps {(x,y):y>0} to R². K instead takes three spatial coordinates and returns a three-component vector. Thus I and II only, original option b. The authentic worked solution explicitly agrees, and saved grading reports Correct. The stored I only key wrongly excludes the permitted restricted-domain field H."} {"question" "q-122697" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "93238ac67f09ef2666247522db1f728c208add278ea3d0d9db55d5065af97927" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "U maps the subset {(x,y,z):x≥y,z>0} of R³ to three-component vectors, so it qualifies under the authentic definition. V maps R² to R², while W is a one-parameter vector-valued curve, not a field assigning vectors to spatial points in R³. Hence I only, original option a. The authentic worked solution confirms this. Saved grading rejected the submitted II only choice; this review is a derived judgment, not a successful grade for option a. The stored III only key is wrong." "bindings" {"problem" "17a71cb155c7a262509e328f0fbe88ac4c4858ed90f3e6a520a0d399a8b3ee43" "worked_solution" "6e7df87911b36de8c0bf535acb7206a84662e61253b9c453609ebe219ef7beb5" "original_field" "80f1509f965fdf912f47ade