a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "G is a one-parameter curve and H maps R² to R², so neither is a vector field on R³ as defined in the source. K maps the subset of R³ with x≠nπ for every integer n to R³; its cotangent restriction is permitted. Thus III only, original option c. The authentic worked solution agrees, and saved grading reports Correct. The stored None of the listed key wrongly excludes K." "bindings" {"problem" "9dd9d50d5ad652aedaa0dd5ea178a90e16a4dda58b50b05f247140b594c853d5" "worked_solution" "d4672ce1fa900ceeb9b4a30cc0d28a63182862e9bc6364a11d9a333ba07de94b" "original_field" "22f920726b09a841193846e896284845b836d3b31dd219bc65865ae1cf845102" "reviewed_field" "7b839696cb06c5a200f77f509296e9425fc9b76d165b188a270ba41c950da552" "raw_widget" "088f5bbc66ce5b547bc0e26dbd68872e66e3c10f9cebbd7877a0b0503ebe1b1a"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-122467-before.png" "sha256" "259ed4d238b87562ce7eea1cf7236645dc8066a39da3cc56087a4ac661093ae6"} {"path" "history-q-122467.png" "sha256" "4af3f76adfc5439148fed84b5d968de55f74964f81d25f8dfd6ba4551a4cb1e7"} {"path" "q-122467-after.json" "sha256" "f9712586d3f488cfcfa6fed8d6c53c221f68daf2e20cbd64d0917e0b16b3ac21"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "0aa1bb836b120f12cf63fe43f86e92beabce23e77bcdac0377887d3d5a689677"}]} {"question" "q-122467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "93238ac67f09ef2666247522db1f728c208add278ea3d0d9db55d5065af97927" "rationale" "G is a one-parameter curve and H maps R² to R², so neither is a vector field on R³ as defined in the source. K maps the subset of R³ with x≠nπ for every integer n to R³; its cotangent restriction is permitted. Thus III only, original option c. The authentic worked solution agrees, and saved grading reports Correct. The stored None of the listed key wrongly excludes K."}] "usage" {"q-122643" [] "q-122697" [] "e-9468" [] "q-122467" [] "e-9465" [] "q-122465" [] "e-9466" [] "q-122518" []} "basis" 1587 "retractions" [[17592186172215 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{P} ( x , y ) = \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + ( x + y {)}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf{Q} ( x , y , z ) = y \\, \\mathbf{i} - x y \\, \\mathbf{j} + ( z + 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{k}$\n3. $\\mathbf{R} ( t ) = ( t + 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( 3 t - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{j}$"] [17592186172210 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{G} ( x , y ) = {x}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{i} - {y}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf{H} ( x , y ) = \\ln   y \\, \\mathbf{i} - {e}^{x y} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf{K} ( x , y , z ) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} - y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k}$"] [17592186172210 :question/answer-fields 17592186168463] [17592186172217 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{3}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{U} ( x , y , z ) = \\langle  x + 2 , \\sqrt{x - y} , \\ln   z \\rangle$\n2. $\\mathbf{V} ( x , y ) = \\langle  x {e}^{y} , 1 - y \\rangle$\n3. $\\mathbf{W} ( t ) = \\langle  {t}^{2} , 2 t , 2 - 3 t \\rangle$"] [17592186172217 :question/answer-fields 17592186168505] [17592186172211 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{3}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{G} ( t ) = {e}^{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} - t \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\sin   t \\, \\mathbf{k}$\n2. $\\mathbf{H} ( x , y ) = {x}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{i} - {y}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf{K} ( x , y , z ) = \\cot   x \\, \\mathbf{i} - y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k}$"] [17592186172211 :question/answer-fields 17592186168469] [17592186172213 :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on $R^{2}$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf{F} ( x , y ) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9 - {x}^{2} - {y}^{2}}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{x y}{{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}} \\, \\mathbf{j} \\, .$."] [17592186171823 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n2. $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n3. $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$"] [17592186171823 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2$. A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It maps every point in the set $S$, given by\n$S = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: \\mid \\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}$\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186171825 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n2. $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n3. $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$"] [17592186171825 :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$. A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}$.\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns every point in the set $S$, given by\n$S = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\: \\mid \\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}$\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186171824 :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,$."] [17592186171824 :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y}\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F$:\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n$x^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0$\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n$D_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}$.\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/2d11f66345e024eed7bf280882ed66a0.png)\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n$1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x$\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n$D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}$.\n\nThe region $D_2$, shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/4792228f471aa7edfa5498ff57e5d63b.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/fd17dd94e4234f271b92358cae0a9b6d.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186168464 17592186168465 17592186168466 17592186168467 17592186168468 17592186168470 17592186168471 17592186168472 17592186168473 17592186168474 17592186168482 17592186168483 17592186168484 17592186168485 17592186168486 17592186168494 17592186168495 17592186168496 17592186168497 17592186168498 17592186168506 17592186168507 17592186168508 17592186168509 17592186168510] "immutable_fields" [[17592186168463 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168463 :answer-field/id] [17592186168463 :answer-field/key] [17592186168463 :answer-field/type] [17592186168469 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168469 :answer-field/id] [17592186168469 :answer-field/key] [17592186168469 :answer-field/type] [17592186168481 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168481 :answer-field/id] [17592186168481 :answer-field/key] [17592186168481 :answer-field/type] [17592186168493 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168493 :answer-field/id] [17592186168493 :answer-field/key] [17592186168493 :answer-field/type] [17592186168505 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168505 :answer-field/id] [17592186168505 :answer-field/key] [17592186168505 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
